=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on **router6**, in its Linux netfilter (iptables) FORWARD chain. Three DROP rules were added on the inbound direction of `eth2` (the `r6_r3` uplink that carries all traffic from the rest of the network toward Location C):

- Rule 2: `DROP tcp --in-interface eth2` — drops all TCP transit
- Rule 3: `DROP udp --in-interface eth2` — drops all UDP transit (confirmed 15 hits, consistent with repeated RIP or other probes)
- Rule 4: `DROP icmp --in-interface eth2` — drops all ICMP transit (4 hits, exactly matching the test pings sent from PC4 to PC15)

Only a single narrow exception exists (rule 1: ACCEPT TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1), which is too specific to be useful in practice. The policy of the FORWARD chain remains ACCEPT, so the DROP rules had to be added explicitly.

The upstream routing is fully healthy: traceroute from PC4 (172.16.10.4) reaches router6's eth2 address (10.50.1.2) in 5 hops via r0→r1→r2→r3→r6. Router6's own routing table has 172.18.51.0/24 directly connected on eth1 (UP), and router6 can itself ping PC4 with zero loss. The sole blocker is the firewall: packets arriving from the WAN on eth2 destined for the Location C LAN (172.18.50/51) are silently dropped before they can be forwarded to eth0/eth1.

**Root cause:** Three `iptables -A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP` rules (for TCP, UDP, and ICMP) were injected on router6, completely cutting off all inbound transit access to Location C.

**Corrective fix:** Remove the three DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain:
```bash
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p icmp -j DROP
```
=== END ===